Au contraire, when you play Nethack you are writing your own 
story, of bravery, cunning, magic and monsters.  Not to mention
the occasional strategic retreat: "I'll be back."


  A story of building strength, constitution, dexterity, wisdom,
and charisma, by such varied ways and means, from such different
starting points that no two tales of character development are
the same.

  A story of companions gained and lost, of being rescued from
certain death by faithful pets.  Some succeed by killing no
creatures themselves.  Others by killing every creature that 
could possibly be.  Some never strike with a wielded weapon,
others wield two at once and inflict outrageous damage with their
every move.

  A visual interface capable of smoothly and consistently 
displaying each of these events could not be made.  The last
game representation that did justice to the spirit of its game
was Doom and its sequels.  And the sound was just as important
and well integrated in those games.  From the pock-pock-pock of
the pistol to the rat-atat-atat-atat of the chain gun to the 
visceral sound of the chain saw you knew exactly how much damage
you were doing based on the sound and the blood splatters.

  The visual position of the monsters on the screen matched up
precisely with the actual position of the monsters.   Ever take
down a cyberdemon with the pistol?  It was accurate enough to
do it.

  There was a hell of a lot of work to make the interface's look
and feel reflect what was happening in the game.  And the depth
and complexity of Doom is nothing compared to Nethack.

  In my mind different color dragons have different 
personalities.  Black are Ancalgon the Black of LotR.  The most
dangerous dragons and therefore the oldest individuals.  White 
are like albino seals, shiny skin, but covering hard muscle 
instead of blubber.  Orange are the bitter outcasts, not 
respected by the more traditional dragons.  Silver and gray
are the youngest individuals, being hunted for their scales
to make armor.

  It would take years to get the full range of animations for
one those dragons to look right to me.  And then it would look
wrong to every one else.

  And what if it was originally a pet dog, polymorphed into
a tame dragon?  How would you represent the image in my mind
of a dog's mind in the body of a dragon.  A pet dog that
alternately frustrated and amused.  "Yes Fido, I want to
push this boulder into the pit.  No, I don't want to use 
that one.  Yes, this one.  Fido, will you just get out of
the way!"  Could you show what I see in my mind when my
pet black dragon Fido drops a quarterstaff at my feet,
begging me to throw it so he can fetch it?

  Or an arch-lich, named "Phydeaux Too", moving with quiet
dignity to kill the Dark One with its cold attack, when I know 
the dog's mind inside is dumbly curious, after the fashion
of dogkind, why it's not biting him?  And what happened to
his paws?


